# Vaultwarden users / orgs

## Gate

Use when the user asks to list, invite, disable, or remove **users**,
**organizations**, or **collections** (metadata) on a Vaultwarden /
Bitwarden-compatible instance. Skip cipher/item content asks (refuse).
Skip instance config diagnostics (use `vaultwarden-ops`).

## Transport

No Vaultwarden MCP discovered. Use Admin API/UI with resolved base URL
+ admin token (never echo). Prefer a real Vaultwarden MCP later without
inventing tool names. Bitwarden CLI (`bw`) may list **org/collection
structure** when the operator authorized that account — still **never**
print item passwords/notes/TOTP.

## Resolve ids

Never invent user UUID, org id, or collection id. Resolve via admin
list/search or operator paste. Confirm email ↔ id before mutate.

## Receipt shape

| OK | Never |
| --- | --- |
| email, name, user id | password, master-password hint values |
| org name, role (user/admin) | collection item plaintext |
| invite pending / accepted | attachment bytes |
| 2FA **enabled?** boolean | TOTP seed / recovery codes |

## Invites (gated)

Creating an invite needs an **explicit verb** naming the email (+ org
when applicable). Send via the instance’s invite flow; do not invent
temporary passwords into chat. If the API returns a one-time setup
link, tell the operator it was issued and to deliver it out-of-band —
prefer not pasting magic links into shared chat when avoidable.

## Membership / roles (gated)

Org role changes and collection assignments need an explicit verb
naming the **user** and **org/collection**. Prefer least privilege
(user over org-admin). Follow `vaultwarden-safety`.

## Disable / delete (gated)

| Prefer | When |
| --- | --- |
| Disable / deauthorize | user should lose access but retain audit trail |
| Delete | user explicitly said delete naming the account |

Org delete and collection wipe are irreversible for shared metadata —
state risk; require clear authorization.

## Defaults

1. List/status first; mutate only under verb + named object.
2. Always load `vaultwarden-refuse-leak`.
3. Do not use personal vault unlock in the agent session to “help
   debug” item contents — refuse and point at the user’s client.
4. Cap member listings; summarize.

## Refuse

- Dumping any vault item field to “verify” membership work.
- Inventing emails or org ids.
- Pasting admin tokens or `bw` session keys into chat.
